course: dictionary
lesson: 15756
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants. The word tripping is a good example. «Mind the tripping hazard by the door.» Here the clue is hazard: tripping means something that can make people fall.
explain: Tripping can mean catching your foot and stumbling. «Sam kept tripping over his untied laces.» The clue is untied laces: his foot caught on them, so tripping means stumbling.
explain: Tripping can also mean turning off suddenly. «The circuit breaker kept tripping during the storm.» The clue is the power: the breaker cut the electricity to stop danger. Always check: does your meaning fit the sentence?
fact: Tripping is the -ing form of trip.
fact: «Tripping» can mean catching your foot on something so that you stumble or fall.
fact: «Tripping someone» means making them fall by putting your foot or something in their way.
fact: A «tripping hazard» is something on the floor, like toys, bags, cables or laces, that can make people fall.
fact: «Tripping over each other» means getting in each other's way.
exercise: «Clear the toys off the stairs, they are a tripping hazard.» What is a tripping hazard? | A place to play football | *Something on the floor that can make people fall | A kind of staircase | A toy that can fly
exercise: «The circuit breaker kept tripping every time we used the kettle.» What does tripping mean here? | Singing loudly while cooking | *Switching off suddenly to cut the power | Stumbling over the kettle | Running fast around the house
exercise: «In the football match, Ella was tripping the other player, so the referee blew the whistle.» What does tripping someone mean? | *Making someone fall by putting your foot in their way | Shaking hands with a friend | Kicking the ball very high | Running past someone very fast
test: «Dad said, "Watch out, you'll keep tripping over that bag."» What does tripping over mean? | Jumping over the bag for fun | Lifting the bag high | Carrying the bag very carefully | *Catching your foot on something and falling
test: «The alarm kept tripping, so the guard came to check the door.» What does tripping mean here? | *Turning on suddenly because of a fault or danger | Ringing softly as a warning | Flying open in the wind | Playing a happy tune
exam: «The children were tripping over each other to get to the front.» What does this mean? | *Getting in each other's way | Winning a race | Singing together | Holding hands while running
